WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.155
Three ways to protect a value you keep.

2
00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:07.960
When a field is needed but sensitive, there are three standard treatments, from strongest to weakest.

3
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.264
Don’t collect it.

4
00:00:09.544 --> 00:00:13.341
If the analysis doesn’t need the user’s email, the log doesn’t get it.

5
00:00:13.621 --> 00:00:23.541
This is the data minimisation principle in GDPR Article 5(1)(c): personal data must be limited to what is necessary for the purpose.

6
00:00:23.821 --> 00:00:24.995
Pseudonymize it.

7
00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:34.320
Replace the identifier with a stable token, so you can still group a user’s records (“this user hit the error five times”) without storing who they are.

8
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:41.021
Use a keyed hash such as HMAC-SHA256, with the key held in your secrets manager.

9
00:00:41.301 --> 00:00:48.619
A plain hash of an email is reversible in practice: an attacker hashes a list of known emails and looks for matches.

10
00:00:48.899 --> 00:01:09.421
Be clear about what this buys you legally: GDPR Article 4(5) defines pseudonymisation as processing that needs separately held additional information to re-identify someone, and recital 26 says pseudonymised data that can be attributed to a person with that information is still personal data.

11
00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:14.736
Pseudonymization reduces risk and exposure; it doesn’t make the log anonymous.

12
00:01:15.016 --> 00:01:15.955
Redact it.

13
00:01:16.235 --> 00:01:21.803
Find sensitive spans inside free text and replace them with a placeholder such as [EMAIL].

14
00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:31.235
This is the only option for content you’ve decided to keep, and it is the least reliable of the three, because it depends on recognizing the sensitive data.

15
00:01:31.515 --> 00:01:33.115
That’s the exercise below.
